INT. PROBATE COURT - CHAMBERS - DAY

A paneled room that smells of lemon polish and old paper. Bookshelves climb to the ceiling, heavy with leather volumes no one has opened in decades. A radiator knocks in the corner. Through a tall window, bare maples shiver against a gray sky.

JUDGE ELEANOR VANDERHOOF, 70s, sits behind a walnut desk with her glasses pushed to the top of her head. Before her, the five heirs of the Whitcombe estate perch on mismatched chairs: the eldest, BRAXTON WHITCOMBE, 50s, in a tailored suit; his sister, CLEMENTINE WHITCOMBE-ADAMS, 40s, twisting a rope of pearls; the lawyer, HORACE PINKNEY, 60s, sweating in tweed; a cousin, ODETTE LAURENT, 30s, silent; and a young man, FINN HARGROVE, 20s, who has just been revealed as the old man's secret son.

                    ELEANOR
The will is clear.

                    BRAXTON
Which one?

                    ELEANOR
The last one.

                    CLEMENTINE
There was no last one.

                    HORACE
There was.

                    BRAXTON
^
There was not!

                    CLEMENTINE
^
Horace, you assured us!

The judge raises one finger. The room obeys.

                    ELEANOR
Mr. Pinkney. Produce it.

He opens his briefcase with trembling hands and draws out a single cream envelope.

Odette, in the corner, finally looks up. Her eyes meet Finn's. She gives the faintest nod.
